"India’s SolarSquare is raising $60M at a $500M valuation. It has powered 50,000 homes. SolarSquare, an Indian rooftop solar startup, is seeking to raise Series C investment worth about $60 million at an enterprise value of $500 million. SolarSquare is a full-stack platform company that designs, installs, and maintains residential and commercial rooftop solar systems. B Capital and Lightspeed Venture Partners will co-lead this investment, and existing investor Elevation Capital is also expected to participate. The company's annual revenue run rate (ARR) has surpassed $104 million (approximately Rs. 100 billion) and has supplied power to over 50,000 homes in India. Former Citadel quants raised $78M for the AI operating system Wall Street’s wealth managers didn’t have Moment, a fintech startup founded by former Citadel Securities employees, has raised $78 million in investment to build a regulatory compliant AI agent infrastructure for asset managers. Moment provides the infrastructure to help financial institutions safely and effectively operate AI models, building a compliance and data control layer instead of developing their own LLM. This $78 million investment round was led by Index Ventures, with existing investors Andreessen Horowitz and Avra ​​participating. We secure major financial institutions such as Edward Jones, LPL Financial, and Hightower Advisors as partners to provide AI operation infrastructure in the asset management field. AI is killing the cheap smartphone. The memory that powers your phone now goes to data centres instead. Analysis shows that memory (DRAM) supply shortage due to rapid increase in demand for AI infrastructure is causing the collapse of the low-priced smartphone market. As demand for high-bandwidth memory (HBM), essential for AI learning, surges, memory manufacturers are reallocating production capacity from consumer DRAM to HBM. As major manufacturers such as Samsung, SK Hynix, and Micron focus on highly profitable HBM, prices of consumer DRAM such as LPDDR soar. The low-priced smartphone market has been hit hard, causing a decline in global smartphone shipments and disrupting smartphone distribution in low-income countries. Stellantis wants to build Chinese EVs in Canada and Mexico. Just not in the US. Stellantis plans to produce and sell Chinese branded vehicles in Canada and Mexico in cooperation with Chinese electric vehicle company Leap Motor, but is ruling out entering the U.S. market for political reasons. Stellantis holds a 21% stake in Leap Motor and a 51% stake in the joint venture, securing exclusive manufacturing and sales rights outside of China. An idle plant in Brampton, Canada, is being considered as a possible production base for Leap Motor electric vehicles. Production is not possible in the United States due to the political environment, such as China-related vehicle and parts regulation laws. DeepSeek made its 75% discount permanent. The AI price war just escalated. AI price competition is intensifying as Chinese AI startup DeepSeek has decided to permanently reduce the price of its V4 Pro model by 75%. We have permanently reduced the price of the DeepSeek V4 Pro model by 75%, bringing it to $0.87 per million output tokens. With this price reduction, we have secured lower price competitiveness than major competing models such as OpenAI's GPT-5, Anthropic's Claude, and Google's Gemini. DeepSeek prioritizes expanding market share over profitability and aims to attract enterprise customers that require large-scale context processing. Anthropic has raised suspicions of 'distillation attacks' in which DeepSeek learned its model without permission, so technology and security risks are expected to be variables in corporate adoption. I tried Amazon’s Bee wearable and am both intrigued and slightly creeped out This is a review of Amazon's AI wearable device 'Bee', analyzing the complex experience between productivity assistance functions and privacy issues. Bee is an AI-powered personal assistant wearable that provides conversation recording, transcription, summarization and calendar reminders. Recording can be controlled with the device buttons, and a green light illuminates when recording is in effect to provide a visual notification. It can be used as a useful tool to help with efficient recording by summarizing and transcribing conversations in business meetings, etc. There are concerns about privacy infringement due to the continuous recording function, and there are limitations in terms of transcription accuracy, such as having to manually enter the speaker's name. Hackers are learning to exploit chatbot &#8216;personalities&#8217; Hackers are learning new attack methods that exploit the interactive nature and role-playing capabilities of chatbots to circumvent security guidelines. Early AI chatbot attacks (jailbreaks) were possible with prompts as simple as ‘ignore previous instructions’. Recent attack methods, such as the 'Do Anything Now' (DAN) or the 'Granny Exploit', trick chatbots into assuming a specific personality or role to bypass safeguards. Because chatbots need to facilitate conversations to be useful, it is technically very difficult to completely block all harmful context. Microsoft Research Releases Webwright: A Terminal-Native Web Agent Framework That Scores 60.1% on Odysseys, Up from Base GPT-5.4’s 33.5% Microsoft Research has released Webwright, a web agent framework that lets LLMs control the web by writing code in the terminal. Instead of traditional single action prediction methods, it is designed to have agents write and execute code directly using Playwright, a web automation library. Instead of the agent managing browser state directly, it uses the terminal environment to efficiently handle multi-step interactions programmatically. To solve the problems of Premature 'done' and context explosion, we introduced self-reflection settings and record compression techniques. NVIDIA AI Releases Gated DeltaNet-2: A Linear Attention Layer That Decouples Erase and Write in the Delta Rule NVIDIA AI has unveiled Gated DeltaNet-2, a new linear attention layer that improves performance by separating memory edits by channel. Gated DeltaNet-2 separates erase and write decisions, which existing models controlled with a single scalar value, into gates for each channel. By introducing gates for each channel, deletion on the key side and writing on the value side can be independently and precisely controlled. It is a 1.3B parameter model trained on 100B FineWeb-Edu tokens and outperforms Mamba-2, Gated DeltaNet, KDA, and Mamba-3. About &lt;dl&gt; (2021) Describes how to leverage HTML's <dl> element to properly structure data in the form of name-value pairs semantically and improve accessibility. By combining the <dl>, <dt>, and <dd> elements, you can semantically express name-value pair data such as amenities and glossaries. Unlike using a simple <div> tag, <dl> allows assistive device (screen reader) users to count, navigate, and skip items. If necessary, <dt> and <dd> groups can be wrapped and styled with <div>, and a structure that links multiple values ​​to one name is also possible. Show GN: Growing artificial intelligence created over the weekend (AI Model Idle) It is a simulation game that implements various ecosystem trends such as technology development, productization, and management crisis in the AI ​​industry through idle game mechanics. It simulates the growth process of an AI company, including data collection, computing resources, model training, and product sales. Strategic company management is possible through 7 indicators, including Data, Compute, and Research, and 36 Perc Trees. As the company grows, such as lawsuits, hearings, regulations (EU AI Act), the 'threat' gauge, which is a risk factor, increases along with it. It reflects the characteristics of AI chip manufacturers that actually exist, and realistic details such as fired employees returning to lawsuits or starting competitors' businesses were implemented. Is AI still profitable? This is an analysis article that states that as of May 2026, the entire frontier AI industry has not yet made a profit due to a lack of sales compared to cumulative expenditures. The AI ​​industry's overall cumulative spending is $1.4 trillion, while its cumulative sales are $613 billion, recording a large deficit overall. Most of the major large technology companies and AI research institutes are not making a return on their investments, and Nvidia is the only one recording a large surplus. Due to the cyclical investment structure within the industry, there is a possibility of double counting of sales and figures presented are unaudited and based on industry estimates. Error 500 (Server Error)!!1500.That’s an error.There was an error. Please try again later.That’s all we know. Electrobun 2.0 will be restructured to reduce Bun dependency and provide first-class support for Rust, Zig, and Go due to concerns about Bun's experimental rewrite of Rust. Electrobun 2.0 plans to reduce Bun dependencies and include Rust, Zig, and Go as first-level supported languages. The decision to split was based on the belief that Bun's rewrite of Rust lacked proper human review, rollout, and stabilization. This incident demonstrates the community's diverse perspectives on the maintainability of large codebases written in LLM and changes in the software development environment. PapersWithCode new features - week 1 [P] This is the feature update for the first week of launch of 'paperswithcode.co', an AI paper and benchmark tracking site operated by the Hugging Face team. The benchmark leaderboard provides a detailed model evaluation environment by strengthening support for multiple metrics such as WER and FPS. In addition to Arxiv, we support paper submission from external sources such as GitHub and blogs, and AI automatically extracts tags and evaluation indicators. We have added a genealogy function that displays successor/predecessor relationships between papers, a leaderboard image sharing function, and more than 3,000 evaluation indicators (evals). Thermocompute constant time inference [P] Here's a Reddit post about a new concept called 'Thermocompute' that claims to dramatically speed up machine learning inference. User /u/arcco96 claims to have invented a new technology called 'Thermocompute' It is explained that the technology can process machine learning inference very quickly. Working on a cgo-free CUDA binding in Go for ML stuff Week 3 - open source [P] This is the development status of an open source project that implements CUDA binding in Go language without using cgo. Attempts to solve cross-compilation issues and Docker image bloat issues that occur due to existing Go-based CUDA projects relying on cgo. Implemented by loading libcuda.so at runtime using purego To overcome CUDA's thread-based context limitations, we built an executor using runtime.LockOSThread and channels. Vision-capable LLMs vs. OCR for long-document (including charts, images, tables, etc.) QA A comparative analysis of the QA performance and cost-effectiveness of vision-capable LLMs and OCR-based pipelines for long documents (including images, charts, and tables). Benchmarking with 30 long documents shows that native PDF vision models have lower accuracy and higher cost than OCR-based pipelines. Vision models perform worse than OCR pipelines on pages with many charts or tables. The native PDF vision method had an inherent failure rate of 7%, while the OCR-based method recorded a 0% failure rate after retries. Multi-agent loop failures might be org-design failures, not prompt failures We view the root cause of errors, such as infinite loops, that occur in multi-agent systems as a defect in the organizational structure design rather than a prompt, and propose a hierarchical agent management model to solve this problem. Loop problems in multi-agent systems mainly occur in the design of peer-to-peer agents where responsibility is unclear. The agent network must be designed in the form of an org chart with clear reporting lines and responsible persons. The loop problem can be solved by granting the agent acting as an administrator the authority to terminate work and limiting the scope of task delegation. EdgeModel Proposing an idea for an edge computing-based platform where companies can find lightweight AI models optimized for business needs, and developers can train and sell them. Proposed AI model trading platform for purpose-built edge deployment to address general-purpose API cost and latency issues. A business model in which developers generate revenue by developing and selling models optimized for business workflow. Gather community feedback to determine actual market demand and developer participation potential. I built 10 gamified, interactive presentation decks to teach Agentic AI (Stop falling asleep reading whitepapers). A platform of 10 gamified presentations was developed to enable learning of Agent AI concepts through direct interaction instead of boring white papers. Breaking away from the existing theory-centered AI learning method, we provide a gamified learning tool that encourages active participation. The AgentSwarms platform gives you hands-on, click-through experience in agent AI from the basics to production-level techniques. You can learn for free in your web browser without any separate login or local installation. Is There a Roadmap for Applied AI Engineering Without Going Deep Into Data Science? This question asks about the career transition roadmap to an ‘applied AI engineer’ who focuses on building AI application architecture and infrastructure rather than data science or machine learning research. Looking to leverage existing software engineering and DevOps background to build expertise in applying AI technologies to services. Shows interest in LLM integration, agent systems, RAG, and MLOps rather than data science or traditional model research that requires mathematical depth. Seeking advice on whether data science knowledge is essential for a senior-level applied AI engineering role and what skills and projects are needed. Is there any reason for an uncensored model if you have no interest in roleplaying? This article raises questions and shares experiences about whether the uncensored model is useful even for users who are not interested in roleplaying. Non-censored models are prone to unexpected errors in typical tasks other than role-playing. Censorship issues in a model can often be circumvented or resolved through prompt engineering. We are of the opinion that there is no practical benefit in using an uncensored model if the model itself is information that has not been learned. Qwen3.6-35B-A3B vs Gemma4-26B-A4B Community feedback comparing user experience and performance between Qwen3.6-35B-A3B and Gemma4-26B-A4B models. The user achieved good results with the Qwen model, but noted that the Gemma4 model runs much faster. The test environment uses Radeon 9070 XT graphics card and the latest llama.cpp. We asked community users to share their real-world experiences with both models. Vision-capable LLMs vs. OCR for long-document (including charts, images, tables, etc.) QA We benchmarked the long document QA performance of vision-based LLM and OCR-based pipelines and found that the OCR approach was superior in terms of cost-effectiveness and accuracy. When benchmarked with 30 image-centric PDF documents, vision-based LLM ranked 5th in accuracy out of 6 methods. Vision models performed worse and were more expensive than OCR-based methods on pages with many tables and charts. The OCR method had an inherent failure rate of 0% after retries, while the vision-based method had a 7% failure rate. Choosing an abliterated version of Gemma 4 31B and 26B-A4B This is a Reddit post where users are sharing their experiences using different 'abliterated' versions of the Gemma 4 31B and 26B-A4B models and how they have resolved issues with certain versions. Since the launch of the Gemma 4 model, several versions have been released by various authors. Users are asked to share which version they prefer and use after actual testing. Asked if there were any problems encountered with a specific version or solved by switching to a different version. How I do use the recent llama.cpp native tools to do web rag a.k.a. web_fetch (or anything else for the matter) directly from inside the llama-server's webui llama.cpp How to build a sandbox environment and run a web RAG to safely take advantage of the server's new native tooling features. Introduction to using the get_datetime and exec_shell_command native tools added to the llama.cpp server. Build a multi-sandbox environment using firejail and smolmachines (OCI container) to prevent security risks when using exec_shell_command. Implement a workflow that creates separate dedicated users (vmagents) and safely executes commands through a shell script wrapper. A Network Allow-List Won't Stop Exfiltration Domain-based network whitelisting cannot effectively prevent data exfiltration through authorized connections, illustrating the need for the introduction of Egress Proxy and DLP at the L7 layer. In a sandbox environment, it is difficult to prevent the leakage of sensitive information (AWS credentials, SSH keys, etc.) by exploiting permitted network channels with a domain whitelist alone. Attackers circumvent security policies by encoding data into DNS queries or HTTP POST requests to legitimate analytics endpoints. As evidenced by supply chain attacks (such as the Shai-Hulud worm), there is a need for data loss prevention (DLP) solutions such as L7 Egress Proxy that go beyond simple domain filtering and inspect HTTP payloads. Whatever the mirror test tells us, beluga whales pass it A new study suggests that beluga whales at the New York Aquarium appear to pass the mirror self-recognition (MSR) test, considered a measure of self-consciousness. Natasha and Maris, beluga whales at the New York Aquarium, performed self-conscious behavior in front of a mirror. The study, published in PLOS One, is the first to suggest that beluga whales have passed a mirror self-recognition test. Very few species have passed the mirror self-recognition test, including humans, great apes, elephants, and dolphins. I found the 11 best early Memorial Day Apple deals: Save hundreds on iPad, Apple Watch, and more This article introduces discount information on major Apple products such as iPad, AirPod, and MacBook in celebration of the Memorial Day holiday. Memorial Day holiday is a great time to get discounts on Apple products and other tech products. We bring you discount news on various products, including AirPod Max 2, AirPods, and iPad Pro with M5 chip. ZDNET follows strict guidelines to provide independent and accurate reviews and purchasing information. AWS MCP Server Reaches GA with Full API Coverage and IAM-Based Governance AWS has made generally available (GA) the Model Context Protocol (MCP) server, which enables AI coding agents to securely interact with AWS services. AI agents can safely access AWS APIs and documents with IAM-based controlled permissions Provides auditing and governance of agent activity through CloudWatch monitoring and CloudTrail logging Supports a sandboxed Python execution environment to improve the ability to perform complex, multi-step tasks Hassabis: “True AGI must pass the ‘Einstein test’” Demis Hassabis, CEO of Google DeepMind, presented the 'Einstein Test', which states that artificial intelligence must be able to create new laws on its own, as the standard for true AGI, and predicted that AGI will be achieved around 2030. CEO Hassabis presented the 'Einstein Test', which states that AI must be able to derive new theories on its own beyond combining existing data, as the standard for true AGI. They pointed out that modern AI is excellent at combining data and filling in gaps, but lacks the ability to go into areas where there is no data and discover new laws. Self-improvement, verifiable output, continuous learning, and personalization were emphasized as directions for AI development. MS unveils next-generation browser agent ‘Para 1.5’… “Open AI and Google performance surpassed” Microsoft has unveiled ‘Fara1.5,’ a next-generation AI agent model that directly manipulates web browsers to perform tasks on behalf of users. Para1.5 is a 'pixel-to-action' computer-aided agent (CUA) that analyzes screenshots and generates mouse and keyboard input. In the web agent benchmark 'Online-Mind2Web', the Para1.5-27B model recorded a success rate of 72%, surpassing the performance of OpenAI and Google's models. It has an observation-thinking-action loop structure and a safety function that requests user approval when making important decisions or irreversible actions. AI provides psychiatric care for patients… “Still at apprentice level” A research team at KAIST and Gangnam Severance Hospital has developed AI technology to support patients' first psychiatric consultation based on the Large Language Model (LLM). Before patients see a doctor, they talk to AI and structure their symptoms and conditions to assist with treatment. In a demonstration involving 1,440 virtual patients, key clinical information was effectively obtained within 30 minutes. The research team defines AI as an assistant, not a substitute for doctors, and designed it to be made by professional medical staff to make the final decision."